  "id": "teamwork_beginner_2",
  "skillId": "teamwork",
  "level": "beginner",
  "title": "Core Concepts & Theories",
  "order": 2,
  "summary": "A shared purpose explains why the team exists and what outcome matters. Goals should be specific enough to guide decisions but meaningful enough to motivate. A weak goal such as \"work on the project\" ",
  "explanation": "### Shared purpose\nA shared purpose explains why the team exists and what outcome matters. Goals should be specific enough to guide decisions but meaningful enough to motivate. A weak goal such as \"work on the project\" can be replaced with \"deliver a tested prototype by Friday that meets the three agreed requirements.\"\n\n### Roles and accountability\nRole clarity reduces duplication and neglected tasks. A responsibility matrix such as RACI can identify who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, and Informed. The model is useful when applied lightly; too much bureaucracy can slow action.\n\nEach task should have a clear owner even when several people contribute.\n\n### Psychological safety\nPsychological safety is the shared belief that people can ask questions, report mistakes, and offer ideas without humiliation or retaliation. It does not mean avoiding standards. A psychologically safe team can challenge weak reasoning while treating people with dignity.\n\n### Stages of team development\nA common model describes forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning. Forming involves orientation; storming includes differences and negotiation; norming establishes working agreements; performing focuses on effective execution; adjourning involves closure and learning. Teams may move backward when membership, goals, or pressure changes.\n\n### Trust\nTrust develops through reliability, competence, care, and integrity. It is built by keeping commitments, communicating early about risks, sharing credit, and admitting errors. Trust is not blind agreement. A trustworthy teammate can respectfully challenge a plan and still support the final decision.\n\n### Diversity and inclusion\nDiverse teams may bring varied experiences, knowledge, and problem-solving approaches. Benefits are not automatic: inclusion is needed so that different perspectives are heard and considered. Leaders can rotate speaking order, share materials in advance, and evaluate ideas on evidence rather than status.\n\n### Conflict\nTask conflict concerns ideas, process conflict concerns how work is done, and relationship conflict concerns personal tension. Task disagreement can improve decisions when managed respectfully. Personal attacks, sarcasm, exclusion, and unresolved resentment usually damage coordination.\n\n### Decision-making\nTeams can use consensus, majority vote, expert decision, or leader decision after consultation. The method should match urgency, expertise, risk, and the need for commitment. Document the decision, assumptions, dissenting concerns, and review date when appropriate.\n\n### Research-informed insight\nTeam performance depends on both expertise and interaction patterns. A team with highly skilled individuals can underperform if information is hoarded, one voice dominates, or members fear speaking. Effective collaboration balances task focus with relationship maintenance.",
