  "title": "Best Practices & Pro Tips",
  "order": 1,
  "summary": "Twenty Recommendations\n\nDefine the problem before solutions.\n\nUse numbers where available.\n\nAsk who is affected and how.\n\nDistinguish symptoms from causes.\n\nWrite assumptions down.\n\nGenerate multiple ",
  "explanation": "### Overview\nTwenty Recommendations\n\nDefine the problem before solutions.\n\nUse numbers where available.\n\nAsk who is affected and how.\n\nDistinguish symptoms from causes.\n\nWrite assumptions down.\n\nGenerate multiple options.\n\nConsider second-order effects.\n\nMatch analysis depth to risk.\n\nUse small experiments for uncertain reversible changes.\n\nSeek disconfirming evidence.\n\nInvite different perspectives.\n\nDocument decision reasoning.\n\nAssign a clear implementation owner.\n\nMeasure the original problem afterward.\n\nWatch for side effects.\n\nStandardize successful changes.\n\nAbandon weak solutions when evidence warrants it.\n\nCapture lessons.\n\nUse factual, non-blaming language.\n\nStay curious when the first explanation feels obvious.\n\n### Quick Wins\nIn your next meeting, replace 'We need to fix this' with a measurable problem statement. Ask one evidence question and one outcome question. Before leaving, assign an owner and next test.\n\n### Advanced Mastery\nAt higher levels, problem solving becomes systems thinking. Look beyond local fixes to incentives, dependencies, feedback loops, and unintended consequences. Also recognize that delay has a cost; not every decision deserves exhaustive analysis.",
