  "summary": "Rate each from 1 (rarely true) to 5 (consistently true): 1) I define problems without embedding a cause. 2) I separate facts from assumptions. 3) I ask questions before proposing solutions. 4) I ident",
  "explanation": "### Fifteen Statements\nRate each from 1 (rarely true) to 5 (consistently true): 1) I define problems without embedding a cause. 2) I separate facts from assumptions. 3) I ask questions before proposing solutions. 4) I identify possible causes. 5) I generate alternatives. 6) I compare options against criteria. 7) I consider risks and constraints. 8) I can run a small experiment. 9) I measure results.\n\n10) I seek disconfirming evidence. 11) I involve relevant stakeholders. 12) I learn from failed attempts. 13) I remain constructive under pressure. 14) I document important decisions. 15) I prevent repeat problems where possible.\n\n### Scoring Guide\nAdd the 15 ratings. 15-34 suggests foundational habits need attention. 35-54 indicates developing capability. 55-67 indicates strong working habits. 68-75 indicates highly consistent practice. This is a reflection tool, not a certification.\n\nChoose your three lowest areas and write one behavior for each to practice over seven days. Reassess after 30 days using concrete examples as well as the score.\n\n### Reflection Prompts\nWhat problem did I solve too quickly? What assumption did I make? Which problem keeps recurring and what evidence suggests a deeper cause? When have I changed my mind because of evidence? What unintended consequence did a solution create? How can I make my next decision more measurable?",
