  "order": 3,
  "summary": "Good reasoning begins with a precise question. Clarify ambiguous terms, define the decision, identify the time horizon, and distinguish a problem from a symptom. Asking \"What exactly are we trying to ",
  "explanation": "### Clarifying and questioning\nGood reasoning begins with a precise question. Clarify ambiguous terms, define the decision, identify the time horizon, and distinguish a problem from a symptom. Asking \"What exactly are we trying to improve?\" can prevent a team from solving the wrong problem. Questions such as \"Compared with what?\" and \"For whom?\" reveal hidden comparison groups and stakeholders.\n\nQuestioning is not interrogation. The tone matters. Use neutral language such as \"What led us to that assumption?\" or \"What else could explain the pattern?\" so that inquiry feels collaborative rather than adversarial.\n\n### Observation and inference\nObservation is what can be directly established from available information; inference is what you conclude from those observations. For example, \"The report was submitted at 5:10 p.m.\" is an observation. \"The employee does not care about deadlines\" is an inference. Mixing the two creates unnecessary conflict because people begin arguing over a conclusion before checking the underlying facts.\n\nPractice by labeling notes with F for fact, I for inference, and A for assumption. In meetings, this simple distinction can dramatically improve clarity.\n\n### Analysis and comparison\nAnalysis breaks a complex issue into parts and relationships. Comparison then tests options against consistent criteria. Useful criteria include cost, quality, risk, reversibility, time, stakeholder impact, and strategic fit. The criteria should be agreed before people become attached to an option.\n\nExample: a student choosing an internship can compare learning opportunities, mentorship, commute, role scope, compensation, and portfolio value. A structured comparison reduces the chance that one attractive feature dominates the decision.\n\n### Inference, evaluation, and conclusion\nInference turns evidence into a provisional conclusion. Evaluation checks whether the evidence is sufficient and whether the reasoning fits. A good conclusion reflects uncertainty rather than hiding it. Words such as \"strongly supported,\" \"plausible,\" \"uncertain,\" and \"unlikely\" can communicate evidence strength more accurately than absolute statements.\n\nThe final sub-skill is revision. New evidence should be allowed to change the conclusion. Changing your mind because the evidence changed is a strength of reasoning, not a failure of consistency.\n\n### Communication of reasoning\nCritical thinking becomes useful to others when the reasoning can be explained. A concise structure is conclusion first, two or three strongest reasons, relevant evidence, uncertainty, and recommended next step. This lets colleagues challenge the reasoning without guessing how you arrived at it.",
