  "title": "Development Strategies",
  "order": 2,
  "summary": "1) Start a daily claim audit. 2) Ask \"What evidence would change my mind?\" 3) Separate facts, inferences, and assumptions in notes. 4) Use a decision matrix for meaningful choices. 5) Practice steelma",
  "explanation": "### Ten practical strategies\n1) Start a daily claim audit. 2) Ask \"What evidence would change my mind?\" 3) Separate facts, inferences, and assumptions in notes. 4) Use a decision matrix for meaningful choices. 5) Practice steelmanning: state the strongest version of an opposing view before responding. 6) Use Five Whys for recurring problems. 7) Run a pre-mortem before major projects. 8) Track predictions and review accuracy later. 9) Read contrasting sources on the same issue. 10) Keep a reasoning journal. 11) Check sample size and representativeness. 12) Label uncertainty explicitly. 13) Ask a subject-matter expert where your reasoning may be technically weak. 14) Teach one reasoning framework to someone else.\n\nThese strategies work best when attached to real decisions. For example, before buying software, list the decision criteria, competing options, evidence, assumptions, and a small pilot. Before accepting a social-media claim, inspect the original source, date, context, and evidence trail.\n\n### Daily and weekly practice\nUse a 10-minute daily routine: choose one claim, identify the conclusion, list two supporting facts, one missing fact, one alternative explanation, and a confidence rating from 0 to 100. Once a week, revisit three judgments and note what changed. This creates an evidence-updating habit rather than a one-time \"critical thinking exercise.\"\n\nFor teams, schedule a 15-minute \"challenge round\" during important decisions. One person summarizes the proposal, another names its strongest evidence, another presents the strongest objection, and the owner states what evidence would cause revision. Keep the session time-boxed so rigor does not become delay.\n\n### 30-60-90 day improvement plan\nFirst 30 days: build awareness. Complete the daily claim audit, learn common reasoning errors, and use fact/inference/assumption labeling on at least three decisions each week. Aim for consistency rather than complexity.\n\nDays 31-60: build application. Use decision matrices, Five Whys, steelmanning, and pre-mortems on meaningful academic or work decisions. Ask one trusted person to critique your reasoning twice per month.\n\nDays 61-90: build mastery. Make prediction logs, conduct post-decision reviews, teach one framework to peers, and measure whether your decisions are becoming faster without becoming less rigorous. Create a personal checklist for high-stakes decisions and refine it after each use.\n\n### Tools and techniques\nUseful tools include spreadsheets for decision matrices, note apps for reasoning journals, collaborative documents for assumption logs, and simple templates for pre-mortems. The important point is not the software; it is externalizing reasoning so that hidden assumptions can be inspected and challenged.\n\nUse a \"minimum sufficient evidence\" rule: define in advance what level of evidence is enough for the decision. Reversible, low-cost choices need less analysis than decisions that are expensive, irreversible, or safety-sensitive.",
