  "order": 1,
  "summary": "Expert recommendations\n\nStart with the decision, not the data.\n\nDefine the claim in plain language.\n\nSeparate facts from interpretations.\n\nUse more than one relevant source for consequential claims.\n\n",
  "explanation": "### Overview\nExpert recommendations\n\nStart with the decision, not the data.\n\nDefine the claim in plain language.\n\nSeparate facts from interpretations.\n\nUse more than one relevant source for consequential claims.\n\nCheck the original source when possible.\n\nAsk what evidence would disconfirm the favored view.\n\nCompare alternatives using explicit criteria.\n\nMatch analysis depth to decision risk.\n\nLook for base rates and context.\n\nAvoid treating a vivid anecdote as a population trend.\n\nState uncertainty rather than hiding it.\n\nSteelman the strongest opposing argument.\n\nUse a pre-mortem for major decisions.\n\nRecord predictions so you can learn from outcomes.\n\nReview decisions after results are known.\n\nInvite respectful dissent in teams.\n\nChange your conclusion when evidence changes.\n\nUse simple frameworks consistently rather than complex frameworks occasionally.\n\n### Common pitfalls and quick wins\nPitfalls include endless research, excessive reliance on experts without checking relevance, confusing disagreement with poor reasoning, and using \"critical thinking\" as a way to delay action. A quick win is to add one sentence to every important recommendation: \"This conclusion would change if\u2026\" That sentence forces explicit uncertainty and evidence thresholds.\n\nAdvanced learners can use sensitivity analysis: identify which assumptions, if changed, would most alter the decision. Focus your research effort on those high-leverage assumptions rather than collecting equal amounts of information about every detail.",
