  "order": 3,
  "summary": "Break outcomes into next actions. \"Finish the report\" is a project outcome; \"open the data file and draft the findings heading\" is a next action. Small, concrete next actions lower start-up friction a",
  "explanation": "### Goal setting and task breakdown\nBreak outcomes into next actions. \"Finish the report\" is a project outcome; \"open the data file and draft the findings heading\" is a next action. Small, concrete next actions lower start-up friction and make progress measurable. For large academic or workplace projects, create milestones tied to deliverables rather than hours spent.\n\nWhen a task remains stuck for several days, the problem may be task definition rather than motivation. Ask what the next physical or digital action actually is.\n\n### Prioritization\nPrioritization combines importance, urgency, impact, effort, and dependency. High-impact tasks with meaningful consequences of delay should usually receive protected attention. Low-value tasks should be simplified, delegated, automated, batched, or removed where possible.\n\nA practical rule is to identify one \"must move\" outcome, two \"should move\" outcomes, and a small list of maintenance tasks for the day. This prevents the to-do list from becoming a false promise to complete everything.\n\n### Scheduling and buffers\nScheduling reserves time for priorities before lower-value work fills the available space. Add buffers between meetings or deep-work blocks when tasks have uncertain duration. A schedule with no recovery or transition time is fragile because any delay propagates through the rest of the day.\n\nFor students, include travel, meals, rest, and revision spacing. For professionals, include administrative time, stakeholder responses, and the unpredictable portion of project work.\n\n### Focus and interruption control\nAttention management includes turning off nonessential notifications, using full-screen focus when possible, closing unrelated tabs, and making communication availability explicit. The goal is not permanent isolation. It is intentional switching instead of continuous interruption.\n\nWhen interruptions are unavoidable, leave a re-entry note before switching tasks: write the last completed step and the next step. This reduces the cost of returning to complex work.\n\n### Communication and boundary setting\nTime management often depends on communication. A professional may need to say, \"I can deliver A today or A and B by Friday; which is more important?\" This turns hidden overload into an explicit priority decision. Clear boundaries are different from refusing to help: they make constraints visible so commitments can be renegotiated intelligently.\n\n### Recovery and energy\nFatigue changes decision quality, attention, and speed. Sustainable time management therefore includes breaks, sleep, movement, and realistic workloads. High output for one week is not the goal if the system causes burnout and missed commitments later. Energy management and time management should be treated as connected but not identical problems.",
