  "order": 4,
  "summary": "Good time management reduces avoidable stress because important tasks receive earlier attention and commitments become visible. It creates a stronger sense of control without pretending that everythin",
  "explanation": "### Personal benefits\nGood time management reduces avoidable stress because important tasks receive earlier attention and commitments become visible. It creates a stronger sense of control without pretending that everything is controllable. People often experience more free time not because the clock changed, but because less time is lost to urgent catch-up, indecision, and rework.\n\nAnother benefit is reliability. When plans are realistic, it becomes easier to keep promises to yourself and others. That reliability can strengthen confidence because confidence grows from repeated evidence that you can manage commitments.\n\n### Professional and career benefits\nProfessionals who prioritize well are better able to meet deadlines, communicate trade-offs, protect high-value work, and respond calmly when priorities change. Managers benefit because visible workload information makes delegation and resource decisions easier.\n\nCareer impact is cumulative. Being known as someone who reliably delivers the important work, rather than simply someone who is always busy, can increase trust and responsibility. The distinction between activity and contribution becomes more important as role complexity increases.\n\n### Case studies\nCase example: a final-year student used to study whatever felt urgent. After a weekly review, the student created protected blocks for difficult subjects, scheduled spaced practice, and reserved one buffer evening. Test scores improved while last-minute study hours decreased because the system moved important work earlier.\n\nCase example: a project coordinator discovered that small meetings occupied the entire afternoon and fragmented analytical work. By grouping short meetings and protecting two morning blocks, the coordinator reduced late-day carryover. The change was not working faster; it was protecting the right kind of attention.",
