  "level": "advanced",
  "title": "Self-Assessment & Reflection",
  "order": 2,
  "summary": "Use the following questions to assess your current presentation habits. Rate each from 1 to 5: 1 = rarely true, 2\n\n= sometimes true, 3 = usually true, 4 = consistently true, 5 = strong habit.\n\nI can s",
  "explanation": "### Overview\nUse the following questions to assess your current presentation habits. Rate each from 1 to 5: 1 = rarely true, 2\n\n= sometimes true, 3 = usually true, 4 = consistently true, 5 = strong habit.\n\nI can state the purpose of my presentation in one sentence.\n\nI identify what the audience already knows.\n\nI organize content around a small number of major ideas.\n\nMy opening explains why the topic matters.\n\nMy slides have clear, informative titles.\n\nMy visuals support rather than compete with my explanation.\n\nI speak at a comfortable pace.\n\nI use pauses and emphasis intentionally.\n\nMy posture and gestures look natural.\n\nI can answer questions without becoming defensive.\n\nI can adapt when time is reduced.\n\nI have a backup plan for technical failures.\n\nI rehearse aloud before important presentations.\n\nI actively seek specific feedback.\n\nI identify one improvement target after each presentation.\n\nScoring guide: add all 15 ratings. A score of 15-30 suggests that presentation habits are still being built and a structured practice routine will be useful. A score of 31-45 indicates a developing foundation with several areas ready for focused improvement. A score of 46-60 indicates consistent capability across many areas. A score of 61-75 indicates strong self-reported habits, although external feedback should still be used to identify blind spots.\n\nReflection prompts:\n\nWhat part of presenting causes the most avoidable difficulty?\n\nWhich audience reacts most positively to your current style?\n\n### Which habit would make the biggest difference if improved?\nDo your slides help the audience or mainly help you remember?\n\n### What evidence would make your message more credible?\nHow would you change the presentation for a nontechnical audience?\n\n### What will you practice before your next presentation?\nTreat the score as a starting point rather than a label. Compare your self-rating with feedback from peers, teachers, managers, or recorded rehearsals. If your self-rating is high but listeners report confusion, investigate the difference rather than defending the score. Improvement comes from observable behavior and audience outcomes.",
