  "title": "Components & Sub-skills",
  "order": 3,
  "summary": "Interview performance consists of several connected components. Career research is the foundation. Candidates should understand the organization, role, industry, products or services, and current resp",
  "explanation": "### Key Concepts\nInterview performance consists of several connected components. Career research is the foundation. Candidates should understand the organization, role, industry, products or services, and current responsibilities described in the vacancy. Research should support relevant conversation rather than produce a memorized company history.\n\nSelf-awareness is the second component. Candidates need a realistic inventory of strengths, development areas, achievements, failures, interests, values, and examples. A useful evidence bank contains academic\n\nprojects, internships, part-time work, volunteering, competitions, leadership activities, customer interactions, technical work, and personal projects where relevant.\n\nRole alignment is the third component. For each important job requirement, identify one or two experiences that demonstrate related behavior. A candidate applying for a software role might map requirements such as programming, debugging, teamwork, documentation, and learning new tools to concrete project examples.\n\nAnswer structure is the fourth component. Behavioral stories benefit from STAR or CAR. Opinion questions benefit from a clear position followed by reasoning and an example. Technical questions benefit from assumptions, method, solution, and verification. The structure should support the question instead of forcing every answer into one template.\n\nActive listening is essential. Listen to the complete question, notice keywords, and take a brief pause before answering. If the question is ambiguous, ask a clarifying question. This is especially important in technical and case interviews.\n\nVerbal communication includes concise sentences, appropriate vocabulary, clear pronunciation, and a professional tone. Candidates should avoid unnecessary jargon unless the interviewer uses or expects it. The goal is to make expertise understandable.\n\nNonverbal communication includes posture, facial expression, eye contact, attentive listening, and natural gestures. In video interviews, looking near the camera during key statements can approximate eye contact, while checking notes constantly can appear distracting.\n\nQuestion handling is another sub-skill. Candidates should distinguish between behavioral, technical, hypothetical, motivation, and role-specific questions. Each type calls for a different response pattern. For example, a behavioral question needs evidence from experience; a technical question may require reasoning; a motivation question needs a credible connection between the candidate and the role.\n\nProfessional etiquette includes punctuality, respectful greetings, appropriate attire for context, careful handling of documents, and courteous follow-up. Etiquette should support professionalism rather than become a performance.\n\nNegotiation and expectation-setting can arise later in the process. Candidates should understand compensation structure, responsibilities, location or schedule expectations, start dates, benefits, and other material conditions before making commitments. Questions should be factual and respectful.\n\nPost-interview reflection completes the system. Record what was asked, what you answered well, where you became uncertain, and which examples could be improved. Over several interviews, this creates a personal learning database.\n\nThese components interact. Research improves role alignment. Role alignment improves answer relevance. Structured answers improve clarity. Active listening prevents irrelevant responses. Professional behavior supports trust. Reflection converts each interview into practice for the next opportunity.",
