  "summary": "Rate each statement from 1 to 5: 1 = rarely true, 2 = sometimes true, 3 = usually true, 4 = consistently true, 5 = strong habit.\n\nI can explain the target role in my own words.\n\nI can identify the top",
  "explanation": "### Overview\nRate each statement from 1 to 5: 1 = rarely true, 2 = sometimes true, 3 = usually true, 4 = consistently true, 5 = strong habit.\n\nI can explain the target role in my own words.\n\nI can identify the top requirements of a role.\n\nI have at least 10 concrete experience examples.\n\nI can structure behavioral answers with evidence.\n\nI can explain my personal contribution to team work.\n\nI can describe a failure and what I learned.\n\nI can answer why this role and why this organization clearly.\n\nI listen fully before answering.\n\nI can handle an unexpected question without relying on a script.\n\nI can explain technical ideas at an appropriate level.\n\nI have thoughtful questions for the interviewer.\n\nI can manage remote-interview technology.\n\nI know how to handle a knowledge gap honestly.\n\nI review my performance after interviews.\n\nI have a specific improvement plan.\n\nScoring: 15-30 indicates that foundations need structured development. 31-45 indicates a developing interview toolkit. 46-60 indicates consistent preparation across many areas. 61-75 indicates strong self-reported habits. These ranges are learning guides, not hiring scores.\n\nReflection prompts:\n\nWhich three experiences best demonstrate your value for your target role?\n\nWhich common question produces your weakest answer?\n\n### Do your examples show results or only activities?\nCan you explain what you personally contributed to team outcomes?\n\nWhich role requirement has the least evidence in your background?\n\nWhat technical or professional gap should you address next?\n\n### Which interviewer question surprised you?\nWhat did you learn about the organization from the interview?\n\n### What one change will you make before the next interview?\nCompare self-assessment with mock-interview feedback. If you believe an answer is clear but reviewers cannot identify your main point, revise the structure. If reviewers say you provide strong evidence but answers are too long, practice shorter versions. The purpose of reflection is observable improvement, not a perfect score.",
