  "summary": "Adaptability supports confidence in unfamiliar situations because the person knows how to learn and adjust rather than needing every condition to be predictable. It can reduce frustration when plans c",
  "explanation": "### Personal benefits\nAdaptability supports confidence in unfamiliar situations because the person knows how to learn and adjust rather than needing every condition to be predictable. It can reduce frustration when plans change because the individual has a practiced response to uncertainty: pause, gather information, test a response, and revise.\n\nIt also broadens identity. A person who sees themselves as a learner can move across roles and technologies without treating every unfamiliar task as a threat to self-worth.\n\n### Professional and career benefits\nEmployers benefit from people who can adopt new tools, work with different personalities, respond to feedback, and maintain performance during change. Adaptable professionals often become useful during transitions because they can learn the new system while helping others translate it into daily practice.\n\nCareer resilience is strengthened when skills are portable. Learning how to learn, communicate change, and solve unfamiliar problems reduces dependence on one specific tool, role, or organizational structure.\n\n### Case examples\nCase example: a marketing associate moves from a campaign team to a product team with different metrics and language. Instead of copying old routines, the associate asks which outcomes matter, learns the product funnel, and changes weekly reporting. The underlying strength is adaptation to a new context while preserving analytical discipline.\n\nCase example: a student's internship becomes partly remote. The student creates a better communication rhythm, documents questions before meetings, learns the shared tools, and asks for feedback on remote collaboration. The change becomes a development opportunity rather than a reason to wait for the old environment to return.",
