  "level": "advanced",
  "title": "Best Practices & Pro Tips",
  "order": 1,
  "summary": "Expert recommendations\n\nProtect the outcome, not every old method.\n\nAsk what is changing and what is not.\n\nSeparate facts from assumptions during transition.\n\nLearn the smallest useful part of a new s",
  "explanation": "### Overview\nExpert recommendations\n\nProtect the outcome, not every old method.\n\nAsk what is changing and what is not.\n\nSeparate facts from assumptions during transition.\n\nLearn the smallest useful part of a new system first.\n\nUse reversible experiments when uncertainty is high and risk is low.\n\nSeek feedback before habits harden.\n\nTreat mistakes as information, then make a concrete adjustment.\n\nKeep a lessons-learned log.\n\nPractice perspective shifting.\n\nCreate contingency options for important goals.\n\nDo not confuse discomfort with danger.\n\nKeep non-negotiable standards clear.\n\nCommunicate what is known and unknown.\n\nAvoid changing everything at once.\n\nWatch for change fatigue and prioritize.\n\nReplace outdated routines explicitly.\n\nCelebrate learning, not just smooth performance.\n\nTeach others what you learned.\n\nReview adaptation outcomes, not intentions.\n\n### Pitfalls, quick wins, and mastery\nQuick wins include learning one feature of a new tool that immediately saves time, asking one clarifying question before reacting to a change, and running one small experiment instead of debating a hypothetical problem. Pitfalls include becoming so flexible that priorities disappear, or becoming so loyal to a preferred method that evidence is ignored.\n\nMastery requires both flexibility and boundaries. The strongest practitioners can say, \"This part is non-negotiable; these three parts are open to experimentation.\" That combination creates adaptability without chaos.\n\n### Immediate actions\nFor immediate practice, choose one current change and write four lines: what is changing, what must stay stable, what you can test this week, and what signal will tell you to adjust. Share the experiment with a peer when collaboration is involved. Small, visible actions are easier to review than broad intentions.",
